1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,310
By 2004, LEGO had invented thousands of new pieces.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:07,790
And most of them were used in exactly one set.

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:14,450
That same year, the company behind the world's most famous toy lost almost two billion Danish kroner.

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,090
About three hundred million dollars.

5
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,050
Those two facts are connected.

6
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:25,150
And the man who would have to untangle them was thirty-five years old, and had joined LEGO only three years earlier,

7
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,750
from a consulting firm.

8
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:34,070
To see how a box of bricks nearly sank its own maker, you have to start with one word that LEGO lived by.

9
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:34,630
System.

10
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:47,490
LEGO began in 1932 as a carpenter's workshop in Billund, Denmark, making wooden toys during the Depression.

11
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:51,510
The name came from two Danish words: "leg godt."

12
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:52,630
Play well.

13
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:59,790
In 1947, the carpenter, Ole Kirk Christiansen, bought a plastic injection-molding machine.

14
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:04,530
It reportedly cost more than twice what the company had earned the year before.

15
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:07,890
By 1949, it was making little plastic bricks.

16
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:16,750
According to LEGO's own history, in 1954 his son Godtfred heard a toy buyer complain that toys had no system.

17
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,350
Nothing connected to anything else.

18
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,410
So Godtfred built one.

19
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:28,010
A town to build, where every piece fits every other piece, and every new set adds to the ones you already have.

20
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:30,050
LEGO launched it the next year.

21
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:32,450
There was just one problem.

22
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:34,630
The bricks didn't hold together very well.

23
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:37,570
So Godtfred added tubes under each brick.

24
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,590
In 1958, LEGO filed the patent.

25
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:42,070
Now every brick locked.

26
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:48,190
Two years later, a fire destroyed the company's warehouse of wooden toys.

27
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:50,050
LEGO didn't rebuild that part.

28
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:51,610
It focused on the bricks.

29
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:54,730
Keep those two ideas in mind.

30
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:58,730
A system where the same pieces keep coming back, set after set.

31
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:02,010
And a company that grew stronger by doing less.

32
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,710
Four decades later, LEGO would forget both.

33
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,450
The system worked for decades.

34
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:17,070
In 1978, the bricks got little figures, and every set became a story.

35
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:21,270
For about fifteen years, sales roughly doubled every five years.

36
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:25,630
The company was now run by the founder's grandson, Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen.

37
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:28,070
Then the ground started moving.

38
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:32,490
The patents on the brick were expiring, and cheaper look-alikes appeared.

39
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,950
Kids were turning to video games earlier.

40
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,090
And the toy business was now decided by giant chains like Walmart and Toys "R" Us.

41
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:43,970
LEGO's answer was more.

42
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:48,610
From 1993, it roughly tripled the number of new toys it put out.

43
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,270
Sales didn't grow.

44
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:51,010
Costs did.

45
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:56,210
In 1998, LEGO lost money for the first time in its history.

46
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:58,150
About a thousand people lost their jobs.

47
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:01,310
And the company started looking for someone to change it.

48
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:10,490
The family brought in a turnaround manager named Poul Plougmann.

49
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:15,610
The diagnosis sounded reasonable: children were changing faster than LEGO was.

50
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:18,010
Maybe a box of bricks wasn't enough anymore.

51
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:22,010
So LEGO set out to become more than a brick company.

52
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:22,790
Theme parks.

53
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:23,310
Clothing.

54
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:24,330
Video games.

55
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:25,170
Television.

56
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:30,050
And, for the first time, other people's worlds: Star Wars sets in 1999.

57
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:31,910
Harry Potter in 2001.

58
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:34,730
At first, it looked brilliant.

59
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:37,590
The Star Wars sets sold enormously.

60
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:41,290
A new line of buildable warriors called Bionicle became a hit.

61
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:48,630
In 2001, LEGO also hired a young economist from McKinsey into its strategy department.

62
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:51,450
His name was Jørgen Vig Knudstorp.

63
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:54,790
For now, he was one person watching the plan from the inside.

64
00:03:55,640 --> 00:04:00,070
And inside, the plan was creating something nobody saw on the shelves.

65
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:02,030
Designers were free to invent.

66
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:04,930
And much of what they invented was new pieces.

67
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:06,930
A special piece for this set.

68
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:08,670
A new color for that one.

69
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:12,170
Every new piece needs its own steel mold.

70
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:13,950
Its own bin in the warehouse.

71
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,610
Its own line in the factory schedule.

72
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:20,530
In the late nineties, LEGO made about six thousand different elements.

73
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:23,450
Within a few years, it was more than twelve thousand.

74
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:27,810
And roughly nine out of ten new pieces were used just once.

75
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:32,610
Every one of those new pieces still clicked onto the old bricks.

76
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,270
That was never the problem.

77
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:39,450
The problem was that most of them were needed just once, and each one still had to be molded,

78
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:40,750
stored and tracked.

79
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:45,790
Some new toys hardly needed building at all.

80
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:48,330
Explore, a line for toddlers.

81
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:52,370
Jack Stone, with big chunky parts that snapped together in minutes.

82
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:59,430
And Galidor, a buildable action figure with its own TV show, video game and fast-food tie-in.

83
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:00,470
It flopped.

84
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:04,050
Why didn't the numbers scream?

85
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:05,510
Because of the movies.

86
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:10,290
New Star Wars films came out in 1999 and 2002.

87
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:13,570
Harry Potter in 2001 and 2002.

88
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:15,550
Each one sold boxes of LEGO.

89
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,530
And covered the cracks.

90
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,910
In 2003, there was no new Star Wars movie.

91
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:28,370
And no new Harry Potter movie.

92
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:32,370
LEGO's sales fell by about a quarter.

93
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:35,030
In the United States, by about a third.

94
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:37,650
More than half of the drop came from the movie sets.

95
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:41,230
And stores that had stocked up the year before now ordered less.

96
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:44,330
That's when the hidden cost showed up.

97
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:49,050
Every one of those thousands of molds, bins and production runs still had to be paid for,

98
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:50,910
whether a set sold or not.

99
00:05:51,960 --> 00:06:00,650
In January 2004, LEGO announced a record loss for 2003: about one point four billion kroner before tax.

100
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:02,435
The turnaround manager left.

101
00:06:02,485 --> 00:06:07,350
Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen took over the day-to-day running of the company himself.

102
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:13,910
When the finance team went through the company product by product, they found something worse.

103
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:18,990
About sixty percent of sales were producing all of the company's operating profit.

104
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:22,350
Everything else, together, was losing money.

105
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:27,350
One finance executive later said LEGO had been destroying value since 1993.

106
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:31,850
And 2004 was turning out even worse.

107
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:37,070
For the first time, it was a real question whether LEGO, as an independent family company,

108
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:38,110
would survive.

109
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:50,710
In October 2004, Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen stepped aside as chief executive.

110
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:55,170
The family handed the company to someone outside the family for the first time.

111
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:57,410
The young man from the strategy department.

112
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:00,090
Jørgen Vig Knudstorp.

113
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:00,990
He was thirty-five.

114
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:06,410
He had spent three years studying how LEGO made and lost money.

115
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:08,450
His first goal was not growth.

116
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:09,930
It was survival.

117
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:13,770
Get cash under control, and cut what the company couldn't afford.

118
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:17,750
The family stayed on as owners, and backed him.

119
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:23,610
Even when the cuts reached Legoland, the park the family had opened in Billund in 1968.

120
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:29,270
In 2005, LEGO sold seventy percent of its four Legoland parks to the investment firm Blackstone,

121
00:07:29,320 --> 00:07:32,050
for three hundred seventy-five million euros.

122
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:35,010
Then the pieces.

123
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:38,150
Designers were told to work with what already existed.

124
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:43,030
The catalog of different elements was cut roughly in half, to about seven thousand.

125
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:48,470
With fewer pieces, the sets went back to what kids actually build: police stations,

126
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:50,170
fire trucks, race cars.

127
00:07:50,820 --> 00:07:54,910
LEGO went back to the big retailers that had called it unfocused and self-centered,

128
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:57,670
and planned its year around what they needed.

129
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:03,330
And it started listening to fans it had mostly ignored: adults.

130
00:08:03,380 --> 00:08:10,810
Years earlier, in 1998, a student had cracked open LEGO's new robot brick and published how it worked.

131
00:08:10,860 --> 00:08:12,590
LEGO didn't sue.

132
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:16,930
In 2005, the new chief executive went to a fan convention himself.

133
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:20,910
It worked faster than almost anyone expected.

134
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:26,050
In 2005, LEGO was back in profit: about five hundred million kroner.

135
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:35,790
But a rescue can make mistakes too.

136
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:41,750
To cut costs further, LEGO moved a large share of its production to a contract manufacturer,

137
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:45,310
Flextronics, in Hungary, the Czech Republic and Mexico.

138
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:47,810
It did not go smoothly.

139
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:50,850
Molds arrived before the factories were ready.

140
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:52,370
Some parts ran short.

141
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:57,490
In 2008, LEGO ended the deal and took production back.

142
00:08:57,540 --> 00:09:03,950
Its operations chief later put the lesson simply: a company has to know what its core competence is.

143
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,350
For LEGO, making the brick was part of it.

144
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,250
Over the next five years, sales grew about a quarter a year.

145
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:20,570
In 2014, a movie made of LEGO bricks earned close to half a billion dollars.

146
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:25,650
In the first half of that year, LEGO passed Mattel and became the biggest toy company in the world.

147
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:29,690
So what nearly destroyed LEGO?

148
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:32,070
Not one bad decision.

149
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:35,070
A changing market pushed it to do more.

150
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:39,250
Doing more filled its factories with pieces that were used once.

151
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:41,130
The movies hid the cost.

152
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:44,630
And when the movies stopped, the cost was all that was left.

153
00:09:45,880 --> 00:09:48,030
The way out wasn't a new idea.

154
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:50,190
It was the oldest one in the company.

155
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:52,810
Fewer pieces, used again and again.

156
00:09:54,260 --> 00:10:00,910
And through all of it, the copies, the losses, the movies, the rescue, one thing never changed.

157
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:03,470
A LEGO brick made today...

158
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:06,290
still clicks onto one made in 1958.

159
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:11,230
The bricks never stopped fitting together.

160
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:13,110
For a few years, the company did.
